<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-diophantine-systems" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Diophantine Systems</title>

  <p> 
    This section concerns diophatine equations and systems and problems about such systems. 
  </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-diophantine-system">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term> diophantine equation </term> defined over a ring <notation> <m>R</m> </notation> 
          is a an equation of the form
        </p>
        <men> P(X_1, X_2, \dots, X_n) = 0 </men>
        <p>
          where <m>P \in R[X_1, X_2, \dots, X_n] </m> is a polynomial.
        </p>
        <p>
          A <term> diophatine system </term> <notation> <m>V</m> </notation> defined over 
          <m> R</m> is a finite disjunction of diophatine equations.
        </p>
            <men> 
              V \, : \bigwedge_{i = 1}^k  \left ( \,  P_i\left (X_1, X_2, \dots, X_n \right ) = 0 \, \right )
            </men>
        <p>
          The <term> <m>R</m>-points </term> 
          <notation> <m> V(R) </m> </notation> of a diophantine system <m> V </m> are those tuples
            <m> (x_1, x_2, \dots x_n) </m> where each <m> x_i </m> is an element of <m> R</m> and each of the
            diophontine equations is satisfied.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      <term> Hilbert's Tenth Problem </term> for the ring <m> R </m> asks
      whether or not there exists an algorithm that takes as input 
      a diophantine system defined over <m> R </m> and outputs either
      <q> Yes </q> if <m> V(R) \neq \varnothing </m> and outputs 
      <q> No </q> if <m> V(R) = \varnothing </m>.

      The following is a celebrated result due to M. Davis, H. Putnam, J. Robinson, and Y. Matiyasevich.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-DPRM">
      <statement>
        <p>
          When <m> R = \Bbb Z</m>, Hilbert's Tenth Problem has no solution. 
          That is, no alogrithm exists to decide whether a system of polynomial
          equations with integer coefficients has an integer solution.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p> 
    For example, we may never know if there is a perfect cuboid, 
    which is a box with integer side lengths, integer face diagonal 
    lengths, and an integer inner diagonal length.
    </p>
  
    <question>
      Do there exist integers <m>a, b, c, \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta</m> such that
      <mdn>
        <mrow> a^2 + b^2 \amp = \gamma^2 </mrow>
        <mrow> b^2 + c^2 \amp = \alpha^2 </mrow>
        <mrow> c^2 + a^2 \amp = \beta^2 </mrow>
        <mrow> a^2 + b^2 + c^2 \amp = \delta^2 </mrow>
      </mdn>
    </question>



    
</section>